            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has considerable amount of air leakage associated with the following locations:

** Crawlspace: Majority of the air leakage is occuring in crawlspace. Please refer to additional comments section of the report for detailed comments on crawlspace. 

** Around the combustion air supply pipe for the pellet stove. I recommend installing a metal plate around the pipe and sealing it with high temperature silicone sealant, which is design especially for high temperature applications.

** The main floor bathroom exhaust fan. It looks like the exhaust fan damper is not sealing well and could be replaced with the new ENERGY STAR* exhaust fan with butterfly damper. 

** Around the attic hatch ( can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")

Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 20% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 340 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 14 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <ceilings>

The attic space of your home appears to have a total of R28 of insulation, comprised of blown cellulose insulation. I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R60. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting.

Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R28 up to a minimum R60, this is estimated to save $ 140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6 GJ, and qualify for a $ 125 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: Our available rebate funding for this year has currently been spoken for. Any new applications will be placed on a waiting list. We can't guarantee that you will receive a rebate.



There are also rebates available for attic insulation throught the Greener Homes Grant, offered by the Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details.</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The crawlspace ceiling located under the steps of the main door appears to have 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation.



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation to the crawlspacel ceiling, you could potentially save $ 27 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1 GJ. 

</ceilingsCathedral>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 1200 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.5 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: Our available rebate funding for this year has currently been spoken for. Any new applications will be placed on a waiting list. We can't guarantee that you will receive a rebate.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 82.1% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 970 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 37 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: Our available rebate funding for this year has currently been spoken for. Any new applications will be placed on a waiting list. We can't guarantee that you will receive a rebate.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT: Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Crawlspace: 



The crawlspace is a major contributor of heat loss in your house and is causing discomfort on the main floor of the house, resulting in cold floor above it. Consider prioritizing the crawlspace when you are planning retrofits to your house.



There are two areas in the crawlspace which require some work from an energy perspective:



1) The crawlspace "ceiling" located under the steps of the main door: During my investigation, I noticed that there is a concrete ceiling with no insulation. Concrete has a high thermal conductivity. and it is acting like a heat sink - absorbing heat from the crawlspace. I would recommend adding at least 3 inches of rigid insulation on this portion of the crawlspace ceiling to slow down the heat transfer. 



2) The area located underneath the front deck is directly exposed to the crawlspace. The crawlspace has a wood framed wall separating this exposed area, however it is poorly insulated, and not air sealed, which is allowing heat to escape very easily. This area was likely meant to be fully sealed (and separated) from the crawlspace itself. We have two potential options for addressing this:

Option A: Properly insulating and air sealing the wood framed wall currently in place which separates these. The wall currently has 2 inches of EPS, and no vapour barrier. I recommend insulating the wood studs with fiberglass batts, and properly air sealing the wall with poly. The poly should connect with the barrier currently installed in the floor (crawlspace ceiling). Another possibility to a conventional insulated wall could be installing a plywood sheathing, and adding sprayfoam. Sprayfoam, when thicker than 2 inches will act as a vapour and air barrier, drastically reducing the air leakage in the crawlspace. 

     -Option A does introduce potential for moisture issues under the deck. As the decks framing appears to be open, which would allow rain/snow to easily enter and build up. A small ventilation exhaust fan may be required to help keep this area dry during the summer months. 

Option B: Another option could be to bring this area into the heated space. This could be accomplished by insulating underneath the deck with R40 batts, and installing a vapour barrier. The deck would ultimately become an exposed flat ceiling, so this should be sealed and weather proofed as well, to avoid weather related moisture from entering. The concrete walls in this area are also directly exposed and should be insulated. I recommend adding 3 inches of XPS. 



There is also some air leakage around the headers of the main floor. As the crawlspace is meant to be heated, it is important to keep heat inside the crawlspace by sealing up all air leakage points. I recommend considering applying spray foam insulation to all the header areas, as it provides a high insulating value.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights  Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment ( bathroom fans, rangehood fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 7.3 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace vent up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 

        - two bathroom fans

        - a range hood

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs  Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



